BLUETOOTH AUDIO EVIDENCE (bluetooth.py): real codecs from ffmpeg + a simulated A2DP link
THE TRACK: synthetic pop (drums, bass, piano, lead), 60 s, 44.1 kHz, 16-bit stereo
1. TOO MUCH DATA: CD quality = 44,100 x 16 bits x 2 channels = 1,411.2 kbit/s
  SBC     :  330.7 kbit/s measured from the encoded bytes = 1/4.3 of CD
  aptX    :  352.8 kbit/s measured from the encoded bytes = 1/4.0 of CD
  aptX HD :  529.2 kbit/s measured from the encoded bytes = 1/2.7 of CD
  AAC     :  272.1 kbit/s measured from the encoded bytes = 1/5.2 of CD (ffmpeg's AAC encoder set to 256k; not Apple's encoder)
  (no quality score: a waveform match ranks a perceptual codec unfairly, and no listening test was run)

2. THE RADIO (simulated 120 s): 79 channels; Wi-Fi on channels 24-46 (23 of 79), busy 55% of the time;
  16 body-blocking moments of 60-320 ms (a phone in a pocket as you turn); 14.5 ms of music per packet,
  one try every 3.75 ms (hops to a new channel each try) = 267 tries/s for 69 packets/s
  without adaptive hopping: 22.1% of tries lost (17.4% outside the body-blocking moments)
  with adaptive hopping: 7.6% of tries lost (0.9% outside the body-blocking moments)

3. THE BUFFER (adaptive hopping on): the headphones play each packet a fixed time after it was made
  buffer   20 ms: 103 glitches in 120 s (  3643 ms of music missing)
  buffer   50 ms:  30 glitches in 120 s (  1553 ms of music missing)
  buffer  100 ms:  19 glitches in 120 s (   755 ms of music missing)
  buffer  150 ms:   9 glitches in 120 s (   261 ms of music missing)
  buffer  200 ms:   3 glitches in 120 s (    44 ms of music missing)
  buffer  300 ms:   0 glitches in 120 s (     0 ms of music missing)

LISTENING (window 2.0-8.0 s, picked as the 6 s whose glitch rate is closest to the 50 ms average: 2 glitches vs 1.5 expected)
  small_buffer.wav: 50 ms buffer, 2 glitches;  large_buffer.wav: 200 ms buffer, 0 glitches (the same moments, 200 ms later)
